# C256 rolling MMA visitation experiment

This private draft changes the order in which independent outputs receive one
K32 contribution. It keeps the winning rolling weight-prefetch schedule. It
is uncompiled, unmeasured, and has no production policy entry.

The motivation and limitations are in the
[v35 CPU analysis](../profile/c256-delivery-v35-cpu/REPORT.md.draft). The largest
measured dependency is still late B data at its first carry consumer. Earlier
three-bank, hint and async-ring experiments lost. Native has some B-reusing
column-major MMA groups, but also M-major/mixed groups; older unrolled project
candidates had column-major groups with NOPs and spills. This experiment does
not assume that traversal explains the native speed gap.

## One executable source change

In the rolled branch of `dense32`, the old visitation is:

```text
for each K0..K7:
    load next B if K<7
    for m0..m3:
        read A[K,m]
        for col0..col3: MMA(out[m,col], A[K,m], currentB[col])
    carry next B if K<7
```

The draft collects those same four A reads first, in the same order, and visits
`col0..col3` outside `m0..m3`. Every output accumulator receives the identical
ascending K0…K7 operand stream. Current/next B loads and carry placement remain
unchanged; no K8 address is formed. W2, normalization and attention reductions,
publication, stores, cache preparation and all seven CTA barriers are unchanged.

Collecting A before the within-K nest is an explicit source scheduling change.
The existing rolling SASS already hoists all four shared loads before the
first MMA, but the new source can still change register lifetimes or scheduling.
Source equivalence establishes unchanged addresses/counts and arithmetic,
not identical compiled load placement. All sixteen A words may remain live
longer than in the incumbent. Register allocation, spills, `.reuse`, NOPs and
the earliest consumer of next B must be checked after a later build.

`tools/draft_window_mma_traversal.py.draft` copies the three original rolling
files. Kernel changes are one exact body replacement plus private identifier
renames; the launcher/API have only identifier/diagnostic renames. The inverse
proof reconstructs all three original files byte-for-byte, including line
endings. The complete suffix beginning at `qkv` is independently checked after
undoing names, so attention math and barriers cannot silently change.

## Scope and evidence

The sole new operator is `_inference_window_block_mma_traversal`, with the same
arguments as `_inference_window_block_vector_prefetch`: C256, SM89+, FP8 weights,
Half or packed FP8 input, packed output, optional raw Half output, any valid
batch/field/phase. The launcher uses the same aligned dual caches and validation;
it directly launches the unique `window_block_mma_traversal_kernel`. It does
not choose a public fallback or implement backward. Half input is a storage
contract for FP8 math, not an alternate FP16 network.

The CPU proof checks every original B byte in the nine dense roles across eight
warps and thirty-two lanes: **589,824 unique bytes**, 36,864 lane-vector requests,
73,728 shared A lane-vector reads and 294,912 lane MMA events. Per warp and
dense call this remains sixteen B-vector requests, thirty-two A-vector reads,
128 MMAs and fifty-six carry words. W2 is excluded from these totals and is
byte-identical source. Negative tests reject changed operands, duplicate loads,
missing events, reversed K, early carries, changed reductions/barriers and a
launcher redirected to the old kernel.

The GPU test draft requires the new registration on supported hardware. Its
planned cases compare independent composition and the winning rolling path,
all phases, batch/tails, Half/packed input, optional raw output, misaligned and
strided guarded storage, exceptional payload agreement with the incumbent,
real checkpoint blocks15/49, invalid inputs and autograd rejection. Graph
tests retain three distinct output pairs, poison every output, change both
state and scale in order1,2,0, assert each mutation and published result changes,
and check all outputs/guards on a nondefault stream. None has run on the GPU.

The frozen bundle passes 29 CPU tests (8 source/schedule tests and 21 benchmark
tests), both normally and with Python optimization enabled. An independent
reviewer reran those checks. The 77 planned GPU cases have only been syntax
checked. Cache preparation is also checked against snapshots taken before its
first call, so a mutated canonical weight cannot become an accepted reference.

The paired benchmark draft compares rolling and the trial on real resident
blocks15/49 at48×84,72×120,136×240, with forward/reverse orders. Optional full
comparison retains the actual public cached route. Every captured output is
held and independently checked outside timing; mutation graphs have explicit
lifetimes and three actual input changes. Preparation and physical-layout
adapters are outside this resident logical-kernel timing. Source/binary/weights
are pinned, and no exporter or policy mutation exists.

Before any promotion: require compiled preservation of the intended permutation,
unchanged dynamic data/MMA counts, no earlier next-B consumer, acceptable
resources and no spills; then exact GPU/sanitizer gates and a repeatable >3%
improvement over rolling in both measurement orders. Faster MMA issue could
instead expose more of the next-B wait. No native speed or85% acceptance claim
follows from CPU evidence or a resident component screen.

Reproduce the draft-only CPU checks:

```powershell
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -B tests/test_window_mma_traversal_cpu.py.draft
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -B tools/window_mma_traversal_proof.py.draft
.\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -B tests/test_benchmark_window_mma_traversal_cpu.py.draft
```

The final bundle manifest and independent review live in
`outputs/v35-mma-traversal-draft/`. Activation/build remains a later root-owned
step after the pinned ordinary scan.
